+0.28), about +1.8\u00b0F since 1918. Wilmington's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.26\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.37 to \u22120.14), about \u22122.7\u00b0F since 1918. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.90\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.56 to +1.24) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.30 to +1.09). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 6 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Wilmington's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.36 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 42.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1929 (61.61 in); the driest, 1930 (23.74 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 3 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}